# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the x64-linux-tsan preset.
#
# Use with:  TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$PWD/tools/tsan.supp" ctest --test-dir build/x64-linux-tsan
# (tools/test.sh does this for you when handed the tsan preset.)
#
# There is exactly one entry, and it must stay that way. A suppression is an admission that a race
# is real at the memory-model level and correct anyway — which is true of a seqlock and of almost
# nothing else. Anything else TSan reports is a bug: fix the code, do not extend this file.
# See docs/LOCKING.md.

# ProcessDataRing is a seqlock, and a seqlock is a deliberate data race.
#
# The RT producer memcpy's a record's payload while readers may be memcpy'ing it out; the torn read
# is *detected afterwards* by re-checking the slot's release-stored sequence word, which is the whole
# protocol (libs/node/process_data_ring.cc). TSan reasons about happens-before edges and has no way
# to see that check, so it reports the payload copy every time — it is not wrong about the race, only
# about the conclusion.
#
# The alternative would be making every payload byte a std::atomic, which would cost the wait-free
# property the RT cycle depends on and buy nothing: the reader already discards a raced copy.
race:mm::node::ProcessDataRing::write
race:mm::node::ProcessDataRing::readRecord
